 "version": 1,
 "name": "Tornado showcase",
 "_note": "ADR-580, the brief's \u00a747 final acceptance test: a small dust devil, a classic tornado, a wide cinematic one, an extremely tall one, a thick cloud one, a thin fast one and a cosmic Tree of Life one -- ALL FROM ONE SYSTEM, to show a general procedural tornado generator rather than a one-off hardcoded effect. They differ only in the values of the same field; there is no per-variant code anywhere.\n\nHOW MANY OF THEM RENDER TODAY: one. `EffectBucket::Tornado` caps at a single live medium, so six of the seven resolve into `AtmosphericCounts::dropped` and contribute nothing. That is a stated limit rather than a bug in this file -- the volumetric slot architecture is what lifts it, and this scene is written for the shape it will have then rather than the shape it has now. Until it lands, `tools/make_tornado_showcase.py` derives one single-variant arm per tornado with a camera framed for it, and the contact sheet of those arms is the acceptance evidence.\n\nThe camera here looks along the line of all seven from the south; it is the shot to render the day the slot array exists.\n\nTHE LAYOUT IS BY SIZE, left to right, and the camera frames the SIX that share a scale. The 70 m dust devil sits at the left end and is a speck from there: a generator whose range runs from a dust devil to a 2400 m column cannot show both at a readable size in one frame, which is a fact about the range rather than a framing failure, and is why the per-variant arms exist.\n\nTHE CLASSIC CONE IS FIRST IN THE ARRAY ON PURPOSE. With one medium slot the survivor is whichever tornado comes first, so the one that renders today is the reference variant. The first two versions of this file came back COMPLETELY EMPTY -- not 'one of seven', nothing at all -- because the dust devil held the slot and was sub-pixel at group distance, and there was no warning in the log because `AtmosphericCounts::dropped` reaches no user interface. A silent drop chosen by array order is exactly the owner's 'if I turn on fog I can't see the vortex', and this file reproduces it on demand.\n\nDO NOT JUDGE THE SYSTEM BY RENDERING THIS FILE TODAY. Rendered now it shows one small, heavily aliased Classic Cone in an otherwise empty frame, and that is two limits compounding rather than one:\n  1. ONE MEDIUM SLOT. Six of the seven resolve into `AtmosphericCounts::dropped` and contribute nothing. The slot architecture lifts this.\n  2. SAMPLING. A frame wide enough to hold an 8.2 km line puts the camera 6.6 km back, and at 256 steps over the resulting march that is a ~58 m sample spacing against an 80 m funnel -- so even the storm that does render is stepped past and comes back in blocky bands. That is the same under-sampling ADR-580 measured on the rope in Phase 2, and it is why the per-variant arms derive their step count from their own narrowest feature instead of taking a fixed one.\nThe acceptance evidence is the seven arms `tools/make_tornado_showcase.py` writes, each framed and sampled by one rule applied to all of them. This file is the \u00a747 deliverable and the shot to render the day the slot array exists; it is not a picture of the effect today.",
